  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 PM EDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\n====== Dense River Valley Fog & Warm Afternoons =====\n\nThere is a mid to upper level low swinging through southern KY and \ncan see the swirl on water vapor imagery. This is just bringing \npassing high level clouds in our SE. Light northeasterly flow is \nkeeping temperatures mild in the upper 60s to low 70s across the \narea. Arguably, the most exciting thing to monitor would be dew \npoints today and for the following days. This will determine how dry \nand comfortable each afternoon will feel, along with the chances of \nfog formation each morning as well. Relative humidity values are \ngenerally bottoming out in the low and mid 30s but will recover to \nnear 100% overnight and into tomorrow morning.\n\nLow temperatures tonight will reach the low to mid 40s and mid to \nupper 30s in our deepest, decoupled river valleys. Just as this \nmorning, dense fog will likely form in river valleys in the east, \nbecoming more patchy dense fog in valleys west of I-65. As we saw \nthis morning, fog will dissipate by late morning, with the latest \nbeing near 10-11 AM EDT in eastern areas. Then by Wednesday \nafternoon, the locked in ridge pattern overhead will lock in sunny \nskies and generally light N-NE flow. As long as the deep low off the \neast coast and troughing over the western US continues, our pattern \nwill remain the same. \n\nWe'll have to monitor the dew points tomorrow as well as this will \nalter fog chances for Thursday morning. Fog may become more \nprevalent by then, but this will be fine tuned between now and \ntomorrow night.\n\n===== Stubborn Ridging and Next Potential Rain Chance =====\n\nAs mentioned before, with troughing on either side of the \ncentral/eastern US, our daily weather won't change much. An Omega \nBlocking pattern seems to really take hold and last through the \nweekend. A weak cold front may push through Thursday morning though \nit is expected to remain so dry there won't be a noticeable change \nin weather besides a shift of winds from the N-NW briefly. PWATs \ngenerally remain under the 30th climatological percentile through \nThursday but as the ridging generally begins to very slowly weaken, \nsoutherly flow will return. This will bring PWATs, temperatures and \ndew points slowly higher over the weekend. Not any big changes are \nexpected from this slow moving pattern as highs go from the low to \nmid 70s by Sunday and Monday. Mornings remain crisp and potentially \npatch-ily foggy, mostly in river valleys. With incremental increases \nin PWATs, a potential cold front from a swinging trough in southern \nCanada may bring a line of broken showers Tuesday afternoon. \nConfidence is very low in this, but it is the largest potential \nchange in the weather pattern with highs mid week in the upper 60s. \nDrought will likely worsen as a response to this pleasant, yet dry \nperiod.",
